{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-159407",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{3})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{3})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{5}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{6}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}-{(\\frac{8}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$ The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle\n\n$$\nS=[0,1]\\times [0,2].\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)=2x$ and $h(y)=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}.$\n\nHence, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{8}{75}\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
